<figure class="boundary" aria-labelledby="r35-boundary-title"><figcaption id="r35-boundary-title"><span>Figure 1</span> Two paths, one row to update</figcaption>
<div class="clients"><div><b>Editor A</b><p>Change <code>left</code> from 1 to 2</p></div><div><b>Editor B</b><p>Change <code>right</code> from 1 to 2</p></div></div>
<div class="document"><b>One stored row</b><pre><code>{`id: 1
doc: {"left": 1, "right": 1}`}</code></pre><p>Both changes acquire a lock on this row.</p></div>
<table><caption>After A commits, what does B send?</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">B’s update</th><th scope="col">Final document</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Replace from its saved old document</td><td><code>&#123;"left":1,"right":2&#125;</code><br/>A’s change lost</td></tr><tr><td>Apply <code>jsonb_set</code> to the current stored document</td><td><code>&#123;"left":2,"right":2&#125;</code><br/>Both changes retained</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p class="caption">An anonymous scratch document, not product attributes. The recorded native check executes A before B to expose B’s stale input. It does not measure concurrent wait time. Different JSON paths do not create independent row locks.</p></figure>
